Short square or squoval nails suit anyone easing back into a work routine after summer, low-maintenance, quick to dry, and still perfectly capable of carrying a rich burgundy or a subtle aura fade. A shorter length also tends to show off cat-eye gel especially well, since the magnetic line reads clearly without competing with a lot of extra nail surface. Anyone specifically drawn to this end of the spectrum will find a full, dedicated set of ideas in the guide to <a href='/blog/september/short-september-nails'>short september nails</a>.

Longer almond or coffin shapes give more room for layered art like tortoiseshell or detailed skittle sets, and they carry a more formal, editorial feel if that is the goal for a particular event or photo. The tradeoff is maintenance, longer nails show growth and wear faster and typically need a touch-up sooner than a short, practical set.

Neither option is more "correct" for the season. What matters more is matching the shape and length to how much daily wear-and-tear your hands actually get this time of year, since that will affect how long any of these looks realistically holds up.

